Patom Theory‚Äôs Support from RRG Linguistics 
John Ball1 
Pat Inc. 
{john}@pat.ai 
April 4, 2020 
Abstract 
The lack of an explicit brain model has been holding back AI. This has led to applications that don‚Äôt model 
language in theory and are ineffective at natural language understanding (NLU). This paper explains Patom 
theory (PT), a theoretical brain model, and its interaction with human language emulation. 
Patom theory explains what a brain does, rather than how it does it. If brains just store, match and use 
patterns comprised of hierarchical bidirectional linked-sets (sets and lists of linked elements), memory 
becomes distributed and matched both top-down and bottom-up using a single algorithm. Linguistics shows 
the top-down nature because meaning, not word sounds or characters, drives language. For example, the 
pattern-atom (Patom) ‚Äúobject level‚Äù that represents the multisensory interaction of things, is uniquely stored 
and then associated as many times as needed with sensory memories to recognize the object accurately in 
each modality. This is a little like a template theory, but with multiple templates connected to a single 
representation and resolved by layered agreement. 
In combination with Role and Reference Grammar (RRG), a linguistics framework modelling the world‚Äôs 
diverse languages in syntax, semantics and discourse pragmatics, many human-like language capabilities 
become demonstrable. Today‚Äôs natural language understanding (NLU) systems built on intent classification 
cannot deal with natural language in theory beyond simplistic sentences because the science it is built on is 
too simplistic. Adoption of the principles in this paper provide a theoretical way forward for NLU 
practitioners based on existing, tested capabilities.

1. Overview 
Researchers often quote: ‚ÄúWe don‚Äôt know how the brain works, so our model works by ‚Ä¶ (insert 
computational, mathematical or statistical model here).‚Äù Not knowing is reasonable, since the human 
brain is complex and has evolved from simpler versions by extending them. And computers do many 
things our brain can do, like arithmetic, so perhaps computers and brains are similar, but that raises an 
obvious question: What if the computational model is the wrong one? 
Perhaps, like the problem of fitting circular orbits to models of our solar system, circles with epicycles 
just can‚Äôt model elliptical orbits nor can computation explain brain function. I propose a brain theory 
based on recognition instead, not computation: one that uses learned patterns in a hierarchy. 
It has two benefits: (a) it can start with a similar objective to early animals, like fish and reptiles and (b) 
the same functionality can scale to human language, provided the biology of the animal (innate genetics) 
connects regions in a brain appropriately so the right things can be learned. I look at language in human 
brains specifically to make the case for the regions needed since other human brain properties, like vision 
and motion, are shared amongst the animal kingdom (that‚Äôs not human-level intelligence). 
This paper is presented in sections to focus on the key issues affecting the theory of how brains work, and 
human brains in particular as the only one that can be used as a template for artificial intelligence 
researchers. Patom Theory (PT) is a theoretical model of brain function that has proven to be effective at 
language tasks together with Role and Reference Grammar (RRG), a modern linguistic framework. The 
tasks include complex sentence understanding in a variety of languages, question-answering conversation 
in context, and language generation to a variety of target languages from a given meaning representation. 4 
www.researchers.one/article/2020-04-04 
I start with an analysis of the gap between public announcements that ‚Äúhuman-level language 
understanding tests‚Äù are being surpassed by machines but no commercial products demonstrate such 
grand claims. Scientifically, goals are being achieved, but we‚Äôre not aiming at the right targets if those 
goals don‚Äôt lead to the result we want. 
Our team‚Äôs success has been demonstrated against the Facebook AI Research team, who is significantly 
larger and with a larger budget. Using PT with RRG in 2017, we showed the effectiveness of a number of 
techniques, like the use of meaning instead of parts-of-speech and the exploitation of semantic sequences 
instead of syntactic ones. Section eight discusses the scientific differences in our model. 
There will be a mix of observation and theory to justify PT‚Äôs model ‚Äì from observations in neuroscience 
to applications from other cognitive science theories, such as linguistics and psychology. Obviously, as 
brains do, emulation in AI must automatically track exquisite levels of detail in volume. Section three 
explores the range of accurate recognition and response implementations that demonstrate some of the 
basic requirements in a language understanding system. 
Our team‚Äôs experience in semantic manipulation along with syntactic manipulation suggests that human 
language may be enabled by a very small part of our brain, while the rest of the brain performs semantic 
and context functions, only. Language understanding, the conversion of words and phrases into meaning, 
including the syntax conversion to semantics, may be complete at the posterior middle temporal gyrus 
(word-level comprehension) and language generation, the conversion of meaning into word sequences 
may only include only the premotor and motor cortex for the larynx, tongue and face, after Broca‚Äôs area 
(whose homologous area in the opposite hemisphere controls speech intonation, rate and rhythm). 
Figure 1. Possible areas for brain's syntax in green based on our team‚Äôs experience of conversion between meaning and syntax. 
For understanding, the meanings of words and literal phrases is sufficient to generate context in our 
system, and MTG ties concepts to words in a human brain. For generation, the syntax of phrases is based 
on precise semantic representations which could be from Broca‚Äôs area. The discussion in section five 
explains the rationale in more detail. 
