The persistent gap between artificial intelligence and human cognition arises not from inadequate computing power but from an inadequate model of the mind.  
For more than half a century, mainstream AI research has relied upon the metaphor of the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{brain as computer}: a sequential, symbol-manipulating device operating through rule-based or statistical inference.  
Yet the biological brain exhibits neither the determinism nor the isolation of such systems.  
It operates as a distributed medium of recursive recognition, continually updating predictions and assimilating sensations into stable semantic attractors.  
The failure to ground cognitive science and AI in such a model has produced systems that can imitate linguistic form without accessing meaning.

John Ball‚Äôs </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{Patom Theory} (PT) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{ball2020patom} emerged from this dissatisfaction.  
Rejecting computation as the explanatory basis for cognition, Ball proposed that the brain stores and reuses patterns---</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{patoms}---in a hierarchy of bidirectionally linked sets.  
Each patom integrates multisensory associations into a single, reusable structure that can be matched both top--down and bottom--up to achieve recognition.  
When coupled with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{Role and Reference Grammar} (RRG) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{vanvalin2005exploring,vanvalin1997syntax}, PT enables language understanding systems to perform meaning-driven interpretation and generation across multiple natural languages, thereby modeling human-like comprehension more closely than syntax-first architectures.

The present essay situates Patom Theory within the more general physical and semantic framework of the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{Relativistic Scalar--Vector Plenum} (RSVP).  
RSVP describes cognition, cosmology, and computation as emergent properties of an entropic recursion between three interacting fields: scalar capacity </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, vector flow </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and entropy density </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(S)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
These fields jointly define an evolving manifold of meaning, wherein coherence arises through the continuous minimization of local entropy.  
From this perspective, the processes that underlie recognition, perception, and linguistic understanding are not algorithmic but thermodynamic: they are the results of recursive smoothing operations that drive systems toward states of maximal coherence.

By deriving Patom Theory from RSVP, we demonstrate that Ball‚Äôs discrete recognition hierarchy represents a quantized limit of RSVP‚Äôs continuous entropic recursion.  
The </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{pattern-atom} corresponds to a local section of the RSVP sheaf of meaning; the bidirectional links between patoms instantiate the same top--down and bottom--up flows represented in RSVP by the reciprocal coupling of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
In this formulation, recognition becomes a local equilibrium condition:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> 0,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
while language generation and comprehension are expressed as entropy-preserving projections:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{syntax}} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{sem}}, S].
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Patom Theory thereby occupies a middle stratum between the physics of entropic recursion and the phenomenology of human cognition, translating continuous informational dynamics into discrete neural and linguistic patterns.

The aim of this paper is therefore twofold: first, to formalize the mathematical derivation of Patom Theory from RSVP, and second, to show that the empirical insights of PT---its hierarchical recognition structures, meaning-first linguistics, and neurocognitive correlates---provide concrete biological validation for RSVP‚Äôs general principles.  
In doing so, we reveal a unified account of mind and meaning, in which the human brain functions as a finite, recursive discretization of the universal plenum of entropic information flow.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
