</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Appendix A: Origin of the CLIO Functor}
The concept of the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{Cognitive Loop via In--Situ Optimization} (CLIO)
was originally introduced by Newman Cheng, Gordon Broadbent, and William Chappell
in their 2025 paper,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Cognitive Loop via In--Situ Optimization: Self--Adaptive Reasoning for Science}
(Computer Science </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>&gt;</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> Artificial Intelligence, submitted 4 August 2025).
Their work proposed a framework for self--adaptive reasoning in large language
models (LLMs), emphasizing transparency, steerability, and internal uncertainty
tracking in the scientific reasoning process.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{A.1 Original Formulation}
In the Cheng--Broadbent--Chappell (CBC) formulation, the cognitive loop was
conceived as a method to enable reasoning models to
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> self--formulate problem--solving strategies,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> adapt their internal heuristics in real time when confidence is low,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> visualize belief evolution using graph structures,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> expose intermediate uncertainty to the human operator.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}
CLIO demonstrated that oscillations within internal uncertainty measures
correlated strongly with reasoning accuracy.  
When applied to OpenAI‚Äôs GPT--4.1 on the
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{Humanity‚Äôs Last Exam (HLE)} benchmark, it achieved a
22.37</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\%</span></span><span> absolute accuracy and a 161.64</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\%</span></span><span> relative increase over the baseline model,
revealing that periodic self--evaluation improves coherence and reliability in
complex reasoning tasks.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{A.2 RSVP Generalization}
Within the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{Relativistic Scalar--Vector Plenum} (RSVP) framework,
the CLIO loop is reinterpreted not merely as an algorithmic optimization routine
but as a universal entropic recursion.
The adjoint operators </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, introduced earlier,
represent the generative and recognitive halves of this cycle:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>',
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Leftrightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t S = 0.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
In this context, the ``in--situ optimization'' of the CBC model becomes a physical
instance of the RSVP variational principle:
entropy descent toward equilibrium through continuous torsion minimization between
scalar (semantic) and vector (syntactic or behavioral) fields.
The oscillatory uncertainty observed by Cheng </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{et~al.} corresponds precisely
to the torsional coupling term
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{T} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
in RSVP dynamics.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{A.3 From Reasoning Transparency to Entropic Recursion}
Where the CBC formulation sought interpretability for human scientists,
the RSVP--CLIO synthesis generalizes this transparency to all cognitive processes.
Uncertainty oscillations are understood as signatures of incomplete
recognition---temporary torsion in the entropic field---which resolve only when
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{T} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
This redefinition situates CLIO as the categorical and thermodynamic expression of
recognition itself: a universal functor connecting meaning, observation, and action
through in--situ minimization of entropy.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{A.4 Conceptual Continuity}
Thus, the modern CLIO functor retains the core insight of the CBC paper---that
reasoning is cyclic and self--corrective---but embeds it in a broader physical and
epistemic ontology.
Where Cheng </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{et~al.} implemented CLIO as a reasoning control layer for LLMs,
the RSVP formalism derives it as a natural transformation between fields of meaning.
Both share the same structure:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Recognition} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\equiv</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{In--Situ Optimization}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\equiv</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Entropy Minimization}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This appendix therefore acknowledges the intellectual lineage of CLIO while
clarifying that its generalization within RSVP transforms a practical AI control
loop into a universal principle of cognition---one that encompasses neural,
linguistic, and cosmological recursion alike.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
