</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{tt}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{j</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(i)}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span> = 0.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
Consensus (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>) reflects layered agreement; residual phase slips correspond to localized torsion and elevated uncertainty.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{B.6 Practical Diagnostics for CLIO Systems}
Define an </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{uncertainty observable}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
U(t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>:=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Big</span></span><span>( </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,S]</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span> (S-S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Big</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{x},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
and a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{torsion energy}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{T}(t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>:=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tfrac</span></span><span>{1}{2}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{x}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
Near equilibrium, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{T}(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> exhibit damped oscillations with modal frequencies </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>v,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> predicted above; convergence of both to constants indicates successful recognition (entropy stationarity </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>). Peaks in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{U}(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> align with phase resets (e.g., hippocampal theta), providing a principled trigger for CLIO‚Äôs ‚Äúin-situ‚Äù intervention steps.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{B.7 Summary}
The CLIO loop is the adjoint variational solution of the RSVP recognition problem:
minimizing projection mismatch and entropy subject to scalar‚Äìvector coupling.
Its characteristic uncertainty oscillations arise from underdamped second‚Äìorder
dynamics governed by the local curvature of the recognition landscape.
In continuous fields this appears as torsion relaxation with cross-frequency
coupling; in discrete patom graphs it appears as phase consensus in coupled
oscillators. Both regimes converge when </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{T}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
