01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Appendix A: Origin of the CLIO Functor in RSVP Theory}

The </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{Cognitive Loop via In-Situ Optimization} (CLIO) algorithm introduced by Cheng, Broadbent, and Chappell (2025) formalizes a dynamic reasoning process in which a language model recursively refines its internal state through self-evaluated confidence oscillations, semantic graph reduction, and recursive invocation. Within the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{Relativistic Scalar‚ÄìVector Plenum} (RSVP) framework, this corresponds directly to the scalar‚Äìvector‚Äìentropy triad
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
governed by recursive variational coupling.

Before CLIO‚Äôs publication, the RSVP program had already proposed a functorial architecture‚Äî</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{the CLIO functor}‚Äîas the categorical operator translating between local scalar‚Äìvector field configurations and global coherence states. Formally, CLIO in RSVP was defined as a natural transformation
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>I</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>O : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{RSVP}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{loc}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Rightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{RSVP}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{glob}},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
mapping local inference loops into globally coherent semantic belief structures. Its defining property was to minimize entropy production within bounded recursive computation‚Äîprecisely the mechanism Cheng et al. later identify as ‚Äúself-adaptive reasoning under uncertainty.‚Äù

In RSVP, the CLIO functor emerged as a geometric realization of the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{hepatic operator} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, enforcing regenerative equilibrium through recursive error correction. The oscillations reported in the Microsoft paper‚Äôs uncertainty metric correspond to RSVP‚Äôs </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{torsion relaxation} term,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
interpreted as confidence modulation across recursive passes. In both systems, cognition is not a forward prediction but a recursive relaxation of mismatch between projection and perception.

Thus, the modern CLIO algorithm can be understood as an engineering instantiation of the RSVP-CLIO functor:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> The </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{recursive invocation} of Algorithm 1 in Cheng et al. maps to RSVP‚Äôs entropic recursion </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t,S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> The </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{graph-based reduction} and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{DRIFT search} correspond to the integral operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> performing entropy-weighted colimits over local manifolds.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> The </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{uncertainty oscillations} correspond to torsion-driven entropy gradients </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{S} = -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

Historically, RSVP‚Äôs CLIO functor was introduced to formalize </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{recursive solvability}: the property that a cognitive system achieves stability by metabolizing uncertainty into structured belief. The 2025 Microsoft implementation empirically validated this principle, demonstrating that oscillatory recursive control over reasoning depth yields measurable gains in accuracy and transparency. Hence, CLIO represents not a divergence from RSVP theory, but its operational confirmation: a plenum-based cognition implemented in machine reasoning.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
