</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:cognitive-ai}

Recent work by John Ball and collaborators on </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{Cognitive AI} and
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{Patom Theory} offers an applied analogue to the recognition-first
principles articulated in the Relativistic Scalar‚ÄìVector Plenum (RSVP)
framework.  Ball‚Äôs thesis, presented in the 2024 Bradford talk, is that
current ‚Äúgenerative AI‚Äù architectures are fundamentally unsustainable:
they scale by brute-force pattern sampling rather than genuine
understanding.  Cognitive AI proposes an alternative built from brain
science and linguistics‚Äîspecifically </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{Role and Reference Grammar}
(RRG)‚Äîin which meaning rather than prediction drives language.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Recognition Before Prediction}
Patom Theory models cognition as the storage and matching of hierarchical
pattern-atoms (Patoms) rather than statistical inference.  Each Patom
encodes a bidirectional linkage between sensory or linguistic features
and their conceptual prototypes, allowing recognition through recursive
agreement rather than generative sampling.  This resonates directly with
RSVP‚Äôs view that cognition minimizes torsion between potential
(</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>) and flow (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>) fields: a Patom match corresponds to
torsion collapse, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, yielding a
low-entropy recognition state.  Where Ball contrasts
‚Äúrecognition vs. prediction,‚Äù RSVP formalizes the same distinction as
entropy descent vs. stochastic expansion.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{RRG as the Linguistic Manifold of Recognition}
Role and Reference Grammar provides the linguistic layer through which
recognition becomes compositional.  Predicates determine their arguments,
constraining combinatorial explosion and mapping directly to RSVP‚Äôs
vector field dynamics: each predicate defines a directed flow among its
roles, while argument realization traces scalar potential gradients in
semantic space.  This replaces syntax-first parsing with field-first
meaning projection, unifying linguistic form, semantic structure, and
contextual interpretation under a single entropic metric.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{From Brain Science to the CLIO Loop}
Cognitive AI‚Äôs architecture separates </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{linguistic skill}
(mapping words </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> meaning) from </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{world knowledge}
(context and facts), mirroring RSVP‚Äôs distinction between local field
configuration and global plenum coherence.  In the CLIO
(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{Cognitive Loop via In-Situ Optimization}) implementation, these
two layers correspond to the adjoint operators
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (projection) and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>*}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (recognition).  Each
recursive pass refines the coupling between syntactic realization and
semantic field alignment, operationalizing Ball‚Äôs pattern-matching
principle as entropic minimization in the RSVP sense.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Sustainability and Interpretability}
By grounding language understanding in explicit predicate‚Äìargument
structures rather than opaque embeddings, Cognitive AI restores both
energy efficiency and epistemic transparency.  RSVP extends this logic to
a physical level: understanding itself is an energy-efficient process,
since recognition lowers entropy by reorganizing structure.  In this
view, Ball‚Äôs call for sustainable, auditable language systems aligns with
RSVP‚Äôs cosmological claim that all enduring systems---from neurons to
civilizations---persist by metabolizing entropy into meaning.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Continuity into the CLIO Experiments}
The experimental appendices thus represent the next stage of this
synthesis.  The EEG/MEG and fMRI studies test the neural correlates of
recognition-first dynamics proposed by Patom Theory, while the CLIO
loop implements these same principles in a computational substrate.
Where Cognitive AI demonstrates how meaning governs language, RSVP +
CLIO demonstrate that meaning governs </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{everything}: language,
perception, and matter alike obey a shared recursion of recognition.
