01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Correspondence Between Role and Reference Grammar and RSVP Theory}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:RRG-RSVP}

Although Role and Reference Grammar (RRG) emerged from linguistic typology and
Patom Theory (PT) from cognitive modeling, their underlying logic aligns
precisely with the Relativistic Scalar‚ÄìVector Plenum (RSVP) framework.
Both treat meaning as a dynamic relation between structure and use rather
than a static symbol map.  The correspondence can be expressed across three
levels: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{semantic geometry}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{functional dynamics}, and
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{entropic recursion}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Semantic Geometry: From Predicates to Potentials}
In RRG, each clause consists of a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{predicate} (an action or state)
and one or more </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{arguments} (participants or entities), linked
through a logical structure such as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{do'}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{John},[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{eat'}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{John},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{cereal})]).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Predicates define the causal topology of the event---who does what to whom,
and how states change.  In RSVP, this same structure appears as a scalar
potential </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> whose local gradients define directed flows
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> between roles:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} = -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{semantic}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Here, the predicate corresponds to the generator of the potential, while
arguments are coordinate axes along which the field flows.
Syntactic variation across languages therefore corresponds to different
coordinate projections of the same underlying semantic manifold.
RRG‚Äôs cross-linguistic generality mirrors RSVP‚Äôs principle of invariance
under coordinate transformation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Functional Dynamics: Linking, Reference, and Vector Flow}
RRG‚Äôs </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{linking algorithm} maps conceptual structures to syntactic
expressions by determining which argument becomes subject, object, or
oblique.  This is a control system ensuring semantic coherence across
morphosyntactic realizations.  RSVP provides a physical analogue: the
vector field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> enforces continuity between local semantic
potentials (conceptual meaning) and global flow patterns (utterance
structure).  Linking is thus the linguistic manifestation of vector
continuity:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} = 0
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Leftrightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{referential consistency}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
In both frameworks, reference is not an external lookup but a maintained
flow of identity through transformations; ‚Äúwho did what‚Äù persists through
changes in tense, aspect, or voice exactly as a conserved current persists
through a field deformation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Entropic Recursion: Recognition and Understanding}
Patom Theory emphasizes that cognition operates through recognition of
pattern-atoms, not prediction of next tokens.  Each Patom is a stored
bidirectional template---a stable attractor in cognitive phase space.  In
RSVP, this corresponds to an entropic basin:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t S = -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where recognition is the moment when torsion between </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> vanishes and entropy locally decreases.  RRG‚Äôs semantic
compositionality implements this recursion linguistically: predicates and
arguments align until semantic torsion is minimized---until meaning
‚Äúclicks.‚Äù  This mechanism explains how comprehension in language mirrors
entropy descent in cognition.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Unified Interpretation: Linguistic Fields as Cognitive Flows}
The resulting correspondence can be summarized as:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{center}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tabular}{lll}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\toprule</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{RRG / Patom Concept} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{RSVP Analogue} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Function}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\midrule</span></span><span>
Predicate (event nucleus) </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Scalar potential </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Source of semantic force</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Argument roles </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Vector field lines </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Paths of reference flow</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Linking algorithm </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}=0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Conservation of identity</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Clause structure </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Local field manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Projection of semantic topology</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Recognition of pattern </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Entropy descent (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{S}&lt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>) </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Alignment / understanding</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Discourse context </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Boundary conditions </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> Global coherence constraint</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bottomrule</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tabular}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{center}
Thus, Role and Reference Grammar provides the formal linguistic
implementation of RSVP‚Äôs recognition dynamics, while Patom Theory
describes the underlying memory architecture that realizes those dynamics
in biological brains.  RSVP unifies both within a single field model,
where meaning, reference, and recognition are three manifestations of one
process: the continuous relaxation of torsion between scalar potential and
vector flow across levels of representation.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
