01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsubsection</span></span><span>*{Worked Example: ‚ÄúAn agent moves an object‚Äù}

To illustrate the correspondence concretely, consider a generic transitive event:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{An agent moves an object.}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{RRG Representation.}
In Role and Reference Grammar, this event decomposes into nested predicates:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{do'}(x, [</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{move'}(x, y)]).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Here </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (the agent) initiates a dynamic predicate </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{move‚Ä≤} that affects
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>y</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (the object).  The predicate encodes a change of location or configuration,
while the arguments specify participants and roles.  The semantic structure
therefore captures both causation and affectedness, independent of word order.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{RSVP Representation.}
In RSVP, the same event is represented by a potential‚Äìflow pair
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> over a semantic manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(x, y) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{move}}(x, y),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} = -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
The agent </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is modeled as the local source of the potential gradient,
and the object </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>y</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as the sink along which the flow occurs.  
The field‚Äôs direction encodes causal transfer (‚Äúfrom </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>y</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>‚Äù), while its
magnitude corresponds to the semantic intensity or degree of motion.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Causal and Aspectual Dynamics.}
The process evolves through time according to
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> = -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}) - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S],
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where the hepatic operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> enforces alignment between the
intended action (agent‚Äôs goal) and the realized outcome (object displacement).
In linguistic terms, this corresponds to successful parsing of the clause
and integration of its meaning into discourse context.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Recognition and Equilibrium.}
When the agent‚Äìobject relation is correctly identified, torsion between
potential and flow vanishes:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{T} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> = 0.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This marks the point of recognition: the cognitive system has matched the
perceptual and linguistic manifolds, producing a low-entropy state
(</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{S}&lt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).  Thus, the comprehension of the sentence
‚ÄúAn agent moves an object‚Äù is the linguistic manifestation of a physical
field‚Äôs relaxation into equilibrium.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Generalization.}
Different verb classes correspond to different potential‚Äìflow geometries:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{transfer} verbs (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{give, send}) ‚Äî two-step flows through an intermediate receiver.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{perception} verbs (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{see, hear}) ‚Äî passive gradients where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> originates externally.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{state} verbs (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{know, believe}) ‚Äî zero divergence fields (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}=0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>), indicating equilibrium.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}
In every case, the RRG decomposition and RSVP field equations describe the
same invariant relation: understanding is the stabilization of directional
semantic flow between participants under entropic constraint.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
