</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:RRG-universals}

The correspondence between Role and Reference Grammar and RSVP theory clarifies
why RRG succeeds as a cross-linguistic model without positing rigid syntactic
universals.  In the RSVP formulation, language is a projection of semantic
field geometry: every utterance corresponds to a local configuration of scalar
potential </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and vector flow </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> constrained by global boundary
conditions.  Variation across languages therefore reflects coordinate
transformations of a single underlying manifold rather than independent
grammatical inventions.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsubsection</span></span><span>*{1. Word Order and Coordinate Transformations}
Languages differ in surface word order (SVO, SOV, VSO, etc.), but this variation
does not alter the semantic topology of an event.  In RSVP terms, each word
order corresponds to a different coordinate projection of the same potential
field:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{agent}}, x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{patient}}, x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{verb}})
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{projection}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span>
[f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{agent}}), f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{patient}}), f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{verb}})].
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
As long as the local gradients </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{agent}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{patient}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> preserve their orientation (who acts on whom),
meaning remains invariant.  Thus, linguistic word order flexibility arises from
the covariance of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> under coordinate transformation‚Äîa direct analog of
general covariance in physical field theory.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsubsection</span></span><span>*{2. Voice and Alignment as Field Symmetry Operations}
RRG explains voice alternations (active, passive, antipassive, causative) as
changes in how arguments are linked to syntactic positions.  RSVP models the
same phenomenon as a symmetry operation on the vector field:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}' = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{S}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{S} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{SO}(n),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{S}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> represents a rotation or inversion of the causal direction
between participants.  Passive voice corresponds to reversing the local flow
(</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>), while causatives introduce an additional
gradient source.  Morphosyntactic voice systems across languages thus emerge
as distinct symmetry breakings of the same underlying semantic flow.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsubsection</span></span><span>*{3. Case and Role Systems as Gradient Topologies}
Cross-linguistic diversity in case marking and role alignment (nominative‚Äì
accusative, ergative‚Äìabsolutive, tripartite) corresponds in RSVP to how
gradients of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are partitioned.  Each case system defines a topological
pattern of connectivity among participants:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{nominative: } </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>y,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{ergative: } </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>y </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Different languages weight the same underlying flux differently, resulting in
alternative but semantically equivalent encodings of agency and affectedness.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsubsection</span></span><span>*{4. Lexical Universals as Field Attractors}
RRG‚Äôs notion of universal lexical decomposition (e.g., </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{kill} =
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{cause(become(dead))}) reflects stable attractor configurations in the
RSVP semantic field.  Certain causal and state transitions‚Äîmotion, possession,
perception, change-of-state‚Äîrepresent low-entropy basins of meaning that
recur across languages because they correspond to physically realizable forms
of energy and information transfer.  Language universals are therefore not
arbitrary but thermodynamically constrained: they mirror the stable modes of
the entropic plenum.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsubsection</span></span><span>*{5. Discourse Structure as Boundary Geometry}
RRG integrates syntax, semantics, and pragmatics through its layered structure,
where discourse context shapes how clauses link together.  In RSVP, discourse
acts as the boundary geometry </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> of the semantic manifold.  Topic,
focus, and information structure modulate boundary conditions on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, while
reference tracking ensures smooth continuation of vector flow across utterances.
Coherence in discourse corresponds to minimal torsion along boundary surfaces:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{n}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{T}|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span>} = 0.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This unifies micro-level linguistic relations and macro-level narrative
structure as manifestations of the same entropic constraint.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsubsection</span></span><span>*{6. The Universal Principle}
Taken together, these correspondences explain RRG‚Äôs success across linguistic
diversity: it reflects not an arbitrary formalism but the natural geometry of
recognition.  The universals of human language arise from the same principle
that governs the cosmos in RSVP‚Äîthe minimization of torsion between potential
and flow.  In both, structure and meaning are invariant under transformation,
bounded only by the requirement that entropy descent remains possible.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
