To visualize the cortical system within the RSVP framework, imagine three nested manifolds, each defined by the interaction between surface expansion and constraint.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{1. The Inner Manifold: Scalar Inflation (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).}
At the core lies a continuously expanding scalar field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(x,y,z,t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, representing proliferative potential‚Äîneural tissue seeking to differentiate and map new representational dimensions.  Its surface tension increases as </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> amplifies, analogous to pressure in an inflating membrane.  This inflation is driven by the biological imperative to maximize surface area for computation and recognition.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{2. The Middle Manifold: Vector Coupling (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).}
Interwoven with this inflationary layer are the long-range axonal vectors </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, which pull distant regions toward functional coherence.  These flows curve and intersect, forming tangential bundles that reduce local shear while maintaining global connectivity.  In this middle layer, the cortex behaves like a tensegrity lattice: expansive scalar pressure balanced by tensile vector coherence.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{3. The Outer Manifold: Boundary Constraint (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).}
The skull provides the outermost surface, a fixed boundary </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> that arrests unbounded expansion.  As </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> continues to grow within this rigid envelope, the manifold folds inward, converting excess curvature into internal complexity.  Each gyrus becomes a recursive indentation‚Äîa topological encoding of how the system internalizes the constraint imposed by its world.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Recursive Nesting.}
These three layers interact recursively.  The inner inflation of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> induces vector realignment (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>), whose accumulated tension deforms the boundary geometry (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>), producing further curvature and thereby new scalar gradients.  The system thus folds upon itself repeatedly, generating fractal curvature that corresponds both to physical gyrification and to the hierarchical deepening of cognitive representation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Cognitive Analogy.}
This morphogenetic recursion is the anatomical analogue of CLIO‚Äôs reasoning loop.  Just as the cortex folds to increase representational depth within finite volume, CLIO folds semantic and epistemic manifolds within bounded computational space.  The skull is the physical constraint that forces biological recursion; finite energy and context length are the computational constraints that force algorithmic recursion.  In both cases, constraint induces intelligence: bounded systems achieve coherence not by expansion, but by folding meaning back upon itself until recognition stabilizes.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
