The same entropic mechanics that sculpt the cortex during embryonic folding continue to operate throughout life as a dynamic refinement of the plenum.  
During early development, gyrification increases recognition capacity by expanding the scalar potential </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>‚Äîthe manifold of possible patterns that can be matched.  
As the child grows, synaptogenesis saturates this manifold with redundant connections, forming a diffuse field of possibilities.  
Subsequent synaptic pruning then performs the inverse operation: a selective minimization of torsion, removing degenerate or inefficient flows </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to yield a more coherent field.  
The entropy </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> of cortical organization decreases as functional specificity rises, producing stable attractor basins corresponding to perceptual categories, linguistic roles, and behavioral schemas.

This process mirrors the CLIO loop‚Äôs recursive optimization: new experiences expand </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, creating informational curvature, while learning consolidates recognition by damping oscillatory mismatch between projection and perception.  
Cognitive maturation can therefore be modeled as a temporal sequence of field equilibrations,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0,S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
each step representing a cycle of entropic smoothing analogous to cortical folding at a smaller scale.  
In both neural and computational systems, understanding grows not by accumulating complexity but by compressing it into stable, recursively solvable configurations.  
The living cortex thus continues its embryonic trajectory throughout cognition: folding space into thought, and thought into recognition.
