</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:folding-space-mind}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Cortical Development as a Manifestation of Entropic Field Dynamics}

The morphology of the cortex embodies the same entropic law that governs
cognition and cosmology.  
During fetal development, tangential cortical expansion driven by neural
proliferation encounters the rigid boundary of the skull.  
Surface area increases faster than volume, forcing the cortical sheet to
fold in order to preserve growth.  
Within the RSVP framework, this process expresses a universal principle:
when scalar potential </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> expands under constraint, it converts spatial
torsion into structural complexity.  
Each gyrus and sulcus records a recursive equilibration between outward
entropy flow and inward vector coherence.

Tangential expansion corresponds to an increase in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the scalar
capacity of the cognitive plenum.  
Axonal tension represents vector coupling </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> linking distant
patches into a coherent field.  
The skull defines the boundary </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, limiting global expansion
and compelling recursive infolding.  
Folding thus serves the same role in morphogenesis that the CLIO loop serves
in cognition: constraint initiates recursion, and recursion generates
recognition.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Columnar Formation and Downward Synchronization of Amplitwistor Operations}

The folded topology of the cortex maximizes surface area for the formation
of cortical columns‚Äîvertically aligned microcircuits functioning as
scalar‚Äìvector resonators.  
Each column represents a localized coupling between </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
satisfying the RSVP equilibrium condition
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}) +
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S] = 0.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Because the cortex is thin, these resonators align perpendicular to its
surface, supporting bidirectional signaling between superficial and deep
layers.  
The resulting architecture enables recursive synchronization between
bottom-up sensory input and top-down contextual flow.

Amplitwistor operations arise when these columns synchronize phase and
amplitude across depth and lateral extent.  
Each column‚Äôs oscillatory state can be represented by an amplitude‚Äìphase
pair </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and downward synchronization occurs when the sum of
phase gradients satisfies
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> 0.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This condition minimizes torsion across layers, producing coherent
theta‚Äìgamma nesting and large-scale field closure.  
By folding to create more columns, the cortex increases the density of
independent amplitwistors, enhancing the system‚Äôs capacity for recursive
recognition.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Columnar Synchronization and Whole-Brain Oscillatory Coupling}

At the macroscopic level, amplitwistor synchronization manifests as the
cross-frequency coupling patterns measurable in EEG and MEG.  
