The RSVP‚ÄìCLIO Observatory can be established through a staged three-phase
program combining targeted experimental validation, open data integration, and
cosmological extension.  
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Phase I (Pilot Validation)} would fund joint neurophysiology‚ÄìAI
experiments to empirically correlate cortical torsion descent with CLIO loop
telemetry, using shared entropy metrics and synchronized instrumentation.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Phase II (Cross-Domain Integration)} would develop the Observatory‚Äôs
open telemetry architecture, federating data from EEG/MEG, fMRI, and
computational systems into a standardized </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> schema with
public APIs and real-time analytic dashboards.  
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Phase III (Cosmological Integration)} would extend these principles to
astrophysical datasets, reanalyzing cosmic microwave background and
large-scale structure archives using entropy flux metrics derived from the
hepatic operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
Collectively, these phases could be supported through a hybrid funding model
combining scientific agencies, philanthropic research foundations, and
industry partnerships seeking sustainable, interpretable AI.  
By the end of the program, the Observatory would provide a global empirical
network capable of measuring recursive coherence across all known scales‚Äî
from cortical columns to cosmic filaments‚Äîrealizing the RSVP vision of a
unified science of recognition.
