pt-[1px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="86">Excellent ‚Äî here‚Äôs a <strong data-start="21" data-end="46">comprehensive outline</strong> for your paper titled something like:</p>
<blockquote data-start="88" data-end="215">
<p data-start="90" data-end="215"><strong data-start="90" data-end="215">‚ÄúFrom Recognition to Recursion: Deriving Patom Theory and Cognitive AI from the Relativistic Scalar‚ÄìVector Plenum (RSVP)‚Äù</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="217" data-end="503">This outline integrates every section we‚Äôve developed so far ‚Äî theoretical, empirical, and infrastructural ‚Äî and orders them in a publishable structure suitable for a high-level interdisciplinary journal (e.g., <em data-start="428" data-end="455">Frontiers in Neuroscience</em>, <em data-start="457" data-end="466">Entropy</em>, or <em data-start="471" data-end="499">Cognitive Systems Research</em>).</p>
<p data-start="505" data-end="664">It‚Äôs divided into <strong data-start="523" data-end="542">four main parts</strong> (Theory, Neurocognitive Mapping, Empirical Framework, and Institutional Vision), ending with Appendices and References.</p>
<hr data-start="666" data-end="669">
<h2 data-start="671" data-end="685"><strong data-start="674" data-end="685">Outline</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start="687" data-end="718"><strong data-start="691" data-end="718">Title Page and Abstract</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="719" data-end="1014">
<li data-start="719" data-end="849">
<p data-start="721" data-end="849">Title: <em data-start="728" data-end="849">From Recognition to Recursion: Deriving Patom Theory and Cognitive AI from the Relativistic Scalar‚ÄìVector Plenum (RSVP)</em></p>
</li>
<li data-start="850" data-end="884">
<p data-start="852" data-end="884">Authors, affiliations, keywords.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="885" data-end="1014">
<p data-start="887" data-end="1014">Abstract summarizing recognition-first intelligence, entropic recursion, cortical morphogenesis, and the RSVP‚ÄìCLIO observatory.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1016" data-end="1019">
<h2 data-start="1021" data-end="1074"><strong data-start="1024" data-end="1074">1. Introduction: Recognition Before Prediction</strong></h2>
<p data-start="1075" data-end="1283">1.1 <strong data-start="1079" data-end="1134">Crisis of Generative AI and the Return to Cognition</strong><br data-start="1134" data-end="1137">
‚ÄÉ- Limits of predictive models (energy inefficiency, hallucination, opacity).<br data-start="1214" data-end="1217">
‚ÄÉ- John Ball‚Äôs argument for <em data-start="1245" data-end="1259">Cognitive AI</em> over <em data-start="1265" data-end="1280">Generative AI</em>.</p>
<p data-start="1285" data-end="1521">1.2 <strong data-start="1289" data-end="1319">The Recognition Hypothesis</strong><br data-start="1319" data-end="1322">
‚ÄÉ- Patom Theory: brains as hierarchical pattern-recognition systems, not computational simulators.<br data-start="1420" data-end="1423">
‚ÄÉ- RSVP reframing: recognition as recursive entropy descent rather than prediction minimization.</p>
<p data-start="1523" data-end="1660">1.3 <strong data-start="1527" data-end="1561">Theoretical Synthesis Overview</strong><br data-start="1561" data-end="1564">
‚ÄÉ- From Patom ‚Üí RRG ‚Üí RSVP ‚Üí CLIO.<br data-start="1598" data-end="1601">
‚ÄÉ- Preview of how recognition scales from cortex to cosmos.</p>
<hr data-start="1662" data-end="1665">
<h2 data-start="1667" data-end="1700"><strong data-start="1670" data-end="1700">2. Theoretical Foundations</strong></h2>
<p data-start="1701" data-end="1929">2.1 <strong data-start="1705" data-end="1756">Patom Theory and Recognition-Based Intelligence</strong><br data-start="1756" data-end="1759">
‚ÄÉ- Pattern-atoms (Patoms) as linked bidirectional sets.<br data-start="1814" data-end="1817">
‚ÄÉ- Distributed matching and hierarchical agreement.<br data-start="1868" data-end="1871">
‚ÄÉ- Relation to perceptual control theory and cue foraging.</p>
<p data-start="1931" data-end="2200">2.2 <strong data-start="1935" data-end="1994">Role and Reference Grammar (RRG) and Semantic Recursion</strong><br data-start="1994" data-end="1997">
‚ÄÉ- Predicates and arguments as formal recognition operators.<br data-start="2057" data-end="2060">
‚ÄÉ- Syntax‚Äìsemantics mapping as constraint propagation.<br data-start="2114" data-end="2117">
