The recognition-first lineage running from early cybernetics through ecological
psychology and meaning-first linguistics stands in tension with the
computational paradigm that came to dominate both neuroscience and artificial
intelligence.  
While cybernetic and ecological theorists viewed cognition as an emergent
property of recursive feedback and environmental coupling, the digital
computational model replaced this with abstract symbol manipulation.
This shift created the foundational divergence that persists today:
brains operate through recursive matching and equilibrium maintenance,
whereas most machine systems operate through prediction and optimization.

In the 1950s, the rise of digital computation coincided with Chomsky‚Äôs
generative grammar, establishing a dual hegemony of syntax and computation.
Both assumed that cognition could be decomposed into rule-based symbolic
processes executed over discrete representations.
By contrast, recognition-based traditions‚Äîfrom Gibson‚Äôs affordances to
Maturana and Varela‚Äôs autopoiesis‚Äîtreated cognition as the continuous
self-referential stabilization of sensory and motor fields.

The current generation of large language models, though vastly scaled,
inherits the same assumptions that guided symbolic AI:
that meaning is an emergent artifact of sufficient prediction accuracy.
This approach yields powerful pattern synthesis but fails at grounded
understanding and contextual coherence.  
It is the modern analogue of mid-century computation‚Äîthe same architecture
of expectation without recognition.

The continuity, therefore, lies not in technology but in error.
Both symbolic AI and statistical AI externalize meaning, treating language
as a combinatorial problem rather than an entropic reconciliation.
The divergence now arises from a reassertion of the recognition-first view:
Patom Theory demonstrates that meaning is retrieved through bidirectional
matching; Role and Reference Grammar shows that syntax is constrained by
semantic role relations; and RSVP formalizes these insights physically,
treating recognition as torsion minimization in an entropic field.
Within this lineage, the CLIO architecture represents the historical turning
point: an explicit return to recursive self-measurement, where the act of
reasoning is itself a recognition process.

Thus, the intellectual history of cognition closes a loop.
The recursion envisioned by early cybernetics finds its completion in a
geometry of recognition, where the brain, language, and cosmos are
different expressions of the same entropic symmetry.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
